Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/5] hw/usb: emulate the xHCI Debug Capability

NOT SUBMITTABLE AS IS. docs/devel/code-provenance.rst says QEMU declines
contributions that include or derive from AI-generated content, and this
series was written by an AI coding assistant (see "Tool use" below). It
is prepared so that it is ready should that policy change, or so that a
human can use it as a reference for an independent implementation. It
also carries no Signed-off-by: the assistant cannot certify the DCO.

The xHCI Debug Capability (xHCI 1.2 section 7.6) lets a host controller
present one of its ports as a USB device to another host, over a USB 3
debug cable. Linux uses it for ttyDBC and early printk, and, in a
proposed series, for a network interface (dbc_net). None of these can be
tested in QEMU today, since no emulated controller has the capability.

With dbc=on, qemu-xhci offers it. usb-dbc-peer is the debug host's end
of the cable: a USB device on any USB bus, attached only while the guest
has DbC enabled, enumerating with the identity and strings the guest
programmed.

  1/5 the emulation and usb-dbc-peer. Off by default, so existing
      machines and their migration are untouched; a controller with
      dbc=on blocks migration.
  2/5 migration: the DbC state goes in a subsection sent when the
      controller has dbc=on. The debug host's controller cannot migrate
      the transfers it had in flight to the cable, so after loading, the
      cable is unplugged and plugged back in, as usb-host does with its
      device; a partly done transfer is given up, as on hardware.
  3/5 splits the xHCI qtest's start-up so a test can set up a second
      controller before the first runs; no change to existing tests.
  4/5 qtests: transfers both ways, disable/enable, and migration.
  5/5 documentation.

Testing:
- the xHCI qtests, the six existing ones and the three new ones, pass;
  3/5 alone passes the six existing ones. The migrate test fails, as it
  should, with the emulation changed to keep partial transfers across
  the replug;
- NixOS VM tests run Linux's own DbC drivers (ttyDBC and dbc_net) on
  6.18.64 and 7.4-rc3 against the emulation, with a Linux debug host on
  usb-dbc-peer: enumeration, traffic both ways at all frame sizes up to
  9004 bytes, a guest paused mid-transfer, and DbC disabled and enabled
  five times under traffic;
- checkpatch.pl is clean but for a MAINTAINERS warning on the new file,
  which "USB: F: hw/usb/*" already covers.
Migration was tested by the qtest only, not with a running Linux guest.

Base: the QEMU 11.1.2 release tarball, not qemu.git master. It would
need a rebase before posting.

Tool use: Claude Code (Claude Opus 5.5) wrote all five patches, the
tests and the documentation. The prompts asked for DbC emulation in
QEMU, to test Linux's DbC drivers without hardware, and then for this
series to be prepared for upstream. A human set the goals and the scope;
the code has had no human review yet.

